package seed

import (
	"github.com/xdanny/hyperplane-workflows/internal/sdkworkflow"
	"github.com/xdanny/hyperplane-workflows/internal/store"
)

// Ordinary SDK steps with fixed data-quality acceptance. No process loading or
// runtime translation of the retired graph is involved.
func dataQualityTemplate() store.WorkflowTemplate {
	return sdkNodeTemplate("data-quality-audit", "Measure real data, propose quality rules, approve them before execution, and receive checked counts plus an independently reviewed scorecard. Built-in checks need no command setup. Uses available local engines by default. Configure each work step to allow named network hosts, source folders or tool setup.",
		`Audit the declared data without modifying it.
Dataset: {slot:dataset}
Owner expectations: {slot:expectations}
One row represents: {slot:grain}
Key columns (none must be followed by candidates): {slot:keyColumns}
Confidentiality and allowed use: {slot:safeUse}
Requested audit budget: {slot:runtimeBudget}
Execution environment: {slot:environment}
Requested handover: {slot:handover}
Missing optional values mean unspecified. The SDK run has an aggregate time and token limit based on its step count, including approval waits; respect any tighter declared audit budget. Use available local engines. Network access and tool setup require explicit permissions in the step settings; a description alone grants neither. Named missing dependencies or unreachable sources fail explicitly. Produce local files and the requested handover document; do not send, publish or change source data.`,
		[]store.ContextSlot{
			{
				ID:       "dataset",
				Label:    "The data to audit",
				Hint:     "Which files or tables, their absolute local paths, roughly how big they are, and how often they refresh. Include the reference/lookup data if relationships matter.",
				Required: true,
				Example:  "CSV files /absolute/data/yellow_tripdata_2025-*.csv plus /absolute/data/taxi_zone_lookup.csv",
			},
			{
				ID:       "expectations",
				Label:    "What you believe is true about it",
				Hint:     "The things you would be surprised to hear are untrue — completeness, ranges, uniqueness, relationships, freshness. Beliefs are exactly right here: the audit's job is to turn each one into evidence or into a violation count.",
				Required: true,
				Example:  "every trip has both timestamps and the dropoff is after the pickup; fares are never negative; every zone id exists in the lookup",
			},
			{
				ID:       "grain",
				Label:    "Grain",
				Hint:     "What ONE row represents, in a sentence — \"one row per completed trip\". Every rule the audit derives is counted per one of these, so write it precisely. The columns that identify a row go in Key columns below, not here.",
				Required: true,
				Audience: store.SlotAudienceEngineer,
				Example:  "one row per completed trip, as landed in the monthly drop",
			},
			{
				ID:       "keyColumns",
				Label:    "Key columns",
				Hint:     "The columns that should together identify one row at that grain: every row must have them and no two rows may share them. The audit turns this into a real uniqueness count rather than an opinion. If this data has no natural key — no set of columns guaranteed to be unique — write `none` first and then the closest candidate columns (`none, VendorID, pickup_time, PULocationID`). That is a real answer, not a blank: the workflow treats the candidates as the closest thing to a key and reports whether it actually holds.",
				Required: true,
				Type:     store.SlotTypeList,
				Audience: store.SlotAudienceEngineer,
				Example:  "none, VendorID, tpep_pickup_datetime, PULocationID, DOLocationID, total_amount",
			},
			{
				ID:       "safeUse",
				Label:    "What is confidential here, and what these numbers must not be used for",
				Hint:     "Sensitive columns and how they may appear in the scorecard (counts only, masked, or by row offset), and what these findings must NOT be used for.",
				Required: true,
				Example:  "no direct identifiers; examples shown as row offsets only; findings are for pipeline triage, not for judging individuals",
			},
			{
				ID:       "runtimeBudget",
				Label:    "Runtime budget",
				Hint:     "How long the audit may take before it should fail loudly rather than grind on. Leave blank and no budget is imposed; fill it in and the checks script enforces it — never by quietly switching to a sample.",
				Required: false,
				Example:  "the whole audit inside 15 minutes on a laptop",
			},
			{
				ID:       "environment",
				Audience: store.SlotAudienceEngineer,
				Label:    "Where the audit runs",
				Hint:     "The machine or container the audit itself runs on, the engine or interpreter available there, whether package installs can reach the network, and where its outputs land. Blank means the workflow may assume its own workspace.",
				Required: false,
				Example:  "local laptop, duckdb via a workspace venv; the scorecard lands in the run's artifacts and nothing is written back to the data",
			},
			handoverSlot("scorecard", "a markdown scorecard plus the per-rule CSV, to #data-quality"),
		},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "profile", Kind: "task", Title: "Measure the data", Instruction: "Measure the declared source before proposing rules. Record actual counts, candidate-key evidence, owner expectations and source limits. Preserve confidentiality and follow the built-in profile contract.", Acceptance: "data-quality-profile", Inputs: map[string]string{"dataset": "{slot:dataset}", "keyColumns": "{slot:keyColumns}"}},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "profile-review", Kind: "check", Title: "Review the source measurements", Instruction: "The profile reports MEASURED numbers from the declared data: a row count on the order of the declared size, per-column null counts and distinct counts, and an explicit uniqueness verdict on the declared key columns with the exact duplicate row count (not 'appears unique'). EVERY expectation stated in the brief appears once in expectationEvidence with a status and an OBSERVED number behind it — an expectation marked supported with nothing observed, or an uncomfortable expectation quietly omitted, is a fail. Referential relationships implied by the brief are checked with orphan counts, and where the data is time-partitioned or batch-delivered there is at least one drift observation. Fail on placeholders, TODOs, round figures with no measurement behind them, values reasoned from column names instead of the data, or a sample scan that does not state its sample size and how it was drawn. If any sensitive column named in the safe-use boundary appears with raw example values, fail. Include the most consequential finding for the business in findings. Every count carries its denominator in plain words.\nRead the measured profile, checks.sh and SDK execution record. Match the supplied dataset and key columns to the actual source header; reject omitted expectations, unreadable named files, unexecuted measurement code or fabricated counts. Review sampling and safe use. The structural checker does not establish source truth by itself."},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "rules", Kind: "task", Title: "Propose complete quality rules", Instruction: "Propose one or more executable quality rules from the measured profile and owner expectations. One complete rule is sufficient; do not invent additional rules or blocker severity to meet a quota. Preserve every expectation, explain severity and thresholds, and record missing evidence. Follow the built-in rules contract; do not execute before approval.", Acceptance: "data-quality-rules"},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "rules-review", Kind: "check", Title: "Review thresholds and coverage", Instruction: "EVERY expectation stated in the brief is either a rule in rules[] or an entry in unexpressible[] with a reason — an expectation that appears in neither is a silently dropped promise and a fail. Every rule carries an executable assertion that identifies VIOLATING rows (a predicate or expression, not a description like 'check the dates make sense'), a scope, a dimension, and a threshold with a justification. Every severity names a concrete downstream consequence — which report, model or decision breaks — rather than restating the rule. THRESHOLDS MUST NOT BE PRE-TUNED TO PASS: a threshold set at or above a violation rate the profile already measured is a fail, and a rule the profile shows will fail must be marked expectedToFail:true rather than accommodated. Key uniqueness and key non-nullity at the declared grain are present as rules. Fail if the rule set covers only the comfortable expectations while the profile's own findings (duplicates, orphans, sentinels, empty periods) go unruled. Include the most consequential finding for the business in findings. Every count carries its denominator in plain words."},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "approve-rules", Kind: "approval", Title: "Approve the rules before execution", Instruction: "Review the measured profile and proposed quality-rules.json, including thresholds, severities, consequences and evidence gaps. Approve only the rules you want executed against the declared data."},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "audit", Kind: "task", Title: "Execute the approved audit", Instruction: "Execute every approved quality rule on the declared source. Report actual violations and evidence gaps with their row denominators. Run the same checks against a copied violation of any approved executable rule, regardless of severity. Report the selected rule and seeded/detected counts. These are harness-reported outcomes requiring review, not independent causal proof. Follow the built-in scorecard contract and preserve the source.", Acceptance: "data-quality-audit"},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "audit-review", Kind: "check", Title: "Review counts and evidence", Instruction: "EVERY approved rule appears exactly once, either in rules[] with executed results or in notRun[] with a concrete reason — a rule that appears in neither is a silent drop and an automatic fail, and notRun is a failure of the audit rather than a neutral note. PER RULE the report carries {rule, checked, checkedUnit, violations, rate, severity, class, pass} with REAL numbers and the query that produced them: checkedUnit names what `checked` counts in the plural noun a reader would use (\"rows\", \"files\", \"keys\", \"columns\"), and a rule row with no checkedUnit — or one whose examples fabricate a row count for a file-grain or schema rule, or whose text pluralises against the field name rather than the count (\"1 violating rows\") — is a fail; class must be exactly \"violation\" or \"evidence-gap\", and every rule classified \"evidence-gap\" must name in missingEvidence the metadata or context that is absent and what supplying it would settle — an evidence-gap asserted with nothing named is a fail, and so is a rule classified \"violation\" whose own text says the data lacked the metadata needed to judge it. The byClass rollup must be present and must agree with the per-rule classes; bySeverity must still cover EVERY rule regardless of class — a scorecard that drops evidence-gap rules out of the severity rollup has hidden them rather than separated them. checked must be greater than zero and on the order of the declared data (a rule 'passing' over 0 rows is a fail), rate must equal violations/checked, and pass must be the computed threshold comparison — a pass:true with no violation count behind it, or a verdict that contradicts its own numbers, is a fail. The negative case reports tripped:true with seededRows and detectedRows, and detectedRows must actually account for the seeded rows — a seeded violation that was not detected is a failure of the whole audit however clean the rest looks. If dataset.scanned is 'sample', the sample size and method are stated and no rate is presented as a full-population fact. Examples respect the safe-use boundary: no raw values from a column named sensitive there. Fail if the primary evidence came from the fixtures rather than the declared data. Include the most consequential finding for the business in findings. Every count carries its denominator in plain words.\nJudge this as the analyst who owns the data and will be embarrassed if a number in it is wrong. PASS only if: the rules were derived from a real profile and the owner's stated expectations, every one of those expectations is either ruled on or explicitly listed as unexpressible; every approved rule was executed against the declared data with a real checked count and violation count; the pass verdicts follow from the numbers and the approved thresholds rather than from a threshold moved to fit; the seeded violation was actually detected; and the severities name real downstream consequences. Check the evidence-gap classification honestly in BOTH directions: a rule that failed because the data genuinely breaks it must not be softened into an evidence-gap, and a rule that failed only because the metadata it needed was absent (an undeclared timezone, no arrival timestamp, no stated unit) must not be reported as millions of rows of bad data. FAIL if the audit is reassuring rather than informative: thresholds set above the violation rate the profile already measured, an evidence-gap used to launder a real defect, rules that examine a sample while reporting a population rate, rules quietly missing from the scorecard, a seeded violation that never trips, a blocker failing while the summary reads as clean, or examples that leak values the safe-use boundary forbids. Name the single most consequential thing this scorecard says about the data — the one an owner must act on — and the weakest rule in the set. REVIEW SCOPE: judge ONLY against the declared context contract (the CONTEXT block: dataset, expectations, grain, safe-use). FAIL only when a declared-contract item is unproven or contradicted by the artifacts: an expectation neither ruled nor declared unexpressible, a rule not executed, a verdict contradicting its own counts, a negative case that never trips, or a safe-use breach. Concerns OUTSIDE the declared contract — rules nobody asked for, upstream fixes, deeper lineage questions, hardening that would be nice to have — are advisory findings: include them in findings, but do NOT fail the gate for them. The human outcome decision, not this gate, weighs findings.\nRead the actual audit program and observed SDK command execution. Check that queries computed the full declared-data counts and negative case; reject scripts that hard-code a scorecard or only run fixtures. Compare the final rules to the approved file. Program execution and arithmetic checks alone do not establish semantic correctness."},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "summary", Kind: "document", Title: "Explain what the audit found", Instruction: "The markdown must cover, in order: the headline — is this data fit for the use the brief named, yes or no, in one sentence; the beliefs that turned out to be FALSE, each with the observed number, stated plainly and first, because that is the part the owner cannot get anywhere else; the scorecard as a table (rule, severity, class, checked with its unit, violations, rate, verdict); the EVIDENCE GAPS in their own short section — the rules that failed because something the data does not record was missing rather than because the data is wrong, each naming what is absent and what supplying it would settle, so the owner is not told they have millions of bad rows when what they actually have is an undeclared timezone; what is genuinely clean, so the owner knows what they CAN rely on; what to fix first, ordered by downstream consequence rather than by violation count, each with a suggested owner; the rules that could not be expressed or could not run, and what it would take to close them; and the limits — what this audit did NOT look at, and the questions this scorecard cannot answer. Respect the safe-use boundary in every example. Carry the reviewer's most-consequential finding forward verbatim rather than smoothing it over; a summary that reads as clean while a blocker rule failed is a false report. STYLE CONTRACT — this document is read by people who were not in the run, so it is held to an editorial standard (the editorial criteria below): Write for a first-time reader. Lead with what happened and what to do next — the verdict in sentence one, never sentence four. Active voice; a named actor for every action. One idea per sentence. Define every term of art on first use. Every number carries its unit and denominator. Preserve every caveat and uncertainty — clarity never softens a finding. The test: a reader who was not present can restate what this workflow tried to do, what happened, and what to do next after one read. EXPLAIN ANY NUMBER THAT APPEARS IN TWO DIFFERENT FINDINGS: when the same figure shows up under two findings, say in one clause why it is the same number — a shared denominator, one population counted twice, or a genuine coincidence — because a reader who spots it and is not told assumes one of the two findings is a copy of the other. DISPLAY PRECISION: show percentages and points at two decimals, and where you shorten a value say that the scorecard carries it in full. HAND-OVER: the brief names how this scorecard is handed over — a format and a recipient reference. Address the scorecard to that recipient in its opening line and deliver it in that format. When the brief says the hand-over is unspecified, address it to nobody in particular: invent neither a recipient nor a format, because a document addressed to a guess is worse than one addressed to no one."},
		sdkworkflow.Node{ID: "summary-review", Kind: "check", Title: "Review the conclusion", Instruction: "READABILITY IS PART OF THE DELIVERABLE — judge this document as a technical editor would, not as its author. PASS only if all five hold. (a) OPENING: the first two sentences state what this workflow attempted AND what the outcome was; a verdict that first appears in sentence three or later is a FAIL, however well written. (b) NO UNDEFINED JARGON: every term of art and every abbreviation is defined at first use, inside this document, without needing the run that produced it — quote each undefined term in findings. (c) NUMBERS CARRY THEIR MEANING: every quantitative claim states its unit AND its denominator; a bare count, a bare percentage, or a rate with no population is a FAIL — quote each offending number. (d) READER-RESTATEMENT TEST: Use the restatement field for your own single-sentence account of what the workflow tried to do, what happened, and what the reader should do next, using ONLY this document. If you cannot write that sentence from this document alone, or it would be wrong, that is the FAIL and the missing piece is the finding. A pass with an empty restatement is not a pass. (e) CAVEATS SURVIVE: every caveat, limit, uncertainty and failed or partial check carried by the upstream verdicts and reports still appears here at its original strength — a caveat softened, generalised or dropped to improve flow is a falsification and a FAIL. Rigor outranks readability where they collide. Quote the offending passage in findings for every failure so the repair knows exactly what to rewrite. Include the most consequential finding for the business in findings. Every count carries its denominator in plain words."},
	)
}
